import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The message digests.
   ==========================================================================================

   **One class and one wrapper** serve five algorithms with an identical eight-method surface, and
   `Hmac` is a namespace of functions per digest *constrained* on that class - so a type missing a
   member is a diagnostic where the instance is written rather than where a template was
   instantiated.

   What is in the module and why each is here:

    | `Crc32` / `Crc32C`      | not a digest at all - a checksum, and the one every archive format wants |
    | `Md5`                   | obsolete for collision resistance and *not* for compatibility - it is what a `Content-MD5`, an RPM header and half the world's file manifests are |
    | `Sha1`                  | the same argument, and one more: MySQL's `mysql_native_password` handshake is `SHA1(SHA1(SHA1(password)))`, and a client that cannot compute it cannot log in |
    | `Sha224` / `Sha256`     | the one to reach for when there is a choice |
    | `Whirlpool`             | no live consumer - a table and forty lines, kept because dropping it costs more to reverse than keeping it, and the one digest here written twice |
    | `Murmur3` / `XxHash32`  | non-cryptographic, for the caller who wants a 32-bit fingerprint and not a promise |

   "Legacy" describes what a *new* protocol should choose, not what a library may drop: MD5, SHA-1
   and Whirlpool each carry a `-- Legacy` line saying what they are still for and what to use
   instead.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The class

   **The dependency runs from the state to its width, and the width is a number** - `n` is 32 for
   SHA-256. So `finish` answers `[U8 *n]`, and a body constrained on the class has both the bytes,
   through the conversion every fixed array gets at a call, and the count, as an ordinary value.

   That is what makes the class usable from a *generic* body, which is the whole of why the width is
   in the head rather than a result type `h` standing for `[U8 *32]`. With an opaque `h` neither
   question could be asked: `Digest(d -> h)` runs from the state to the result, so a function taking
   an `h` has nothing to select an instance from, and the module carried a second spelling of
   `finish` routed through `d` - `finishInto(->self: d, &pad: [U8 *64]) -> Size` - implemented
   identically in all nine instances so that `Hmac.of` could reach a digest's bytes at all.

   `class (Contiguous(h, U8)) Digest(d -> h)` is the other way to have closed it and does not work:
   it fails in a build with specialization declined, and the note beside `Length([a *n])` in
   `Core/Container.yana` says why. Naming the width needs no instance at all.

   `finish` takes `->self` deliberately: a digest state is *spent* by finalizing, and leaving a
   reusable-looking object behind is a trap.
-}
pub class Digest(d -> n: Int):
  fn newDigest() -> d
  fn update(&self: d, from: [U8]) -> {}
  fn finish(->self: d) -> [U8 *n]

{-
   The result widths, as types.

   Each is written once and used everywhere the width is a *type* - `finish`'s result, the one-shot
   function's, `Hmac`'s. The number stays written in exactly one place per digest, the instance head,
   which is the one position that takes a count rather than a type; before this it was `[U8 *32]` in
   five places per digest with nothing relating them, and the two SHA-2 widths sit within a few lines
   of each other.

   `Fingerprint` is one name for the four thirty-two-bit results rather than four names for one
   width, because that is what they are: `crc32`, `crc32c`, `murmur3` and `xxHash32` all answer "a
   32-bit fingerprint and not a promise", and a caller storing one has no reason to care which
   produced it. The cryptographic digests get a name each, because a `Sha256Digest` where a
   `Sha1Digest` was meant is a mistake worth a type error.
-}
pub alias Fingerprint = [U8 *4]

{-
   Sixty-four bytes, as a type rather than as a buffer somebody passes a length with.

   `[U8 *64]` is one pointer at a call and a compile-time sixty-four inside the callee, so the loop
   below has no length to load and its bound folds; a `Flat(U8)` would carry a second word saying
   what the type already says, and would let a caller hand over a buffer of a different size for
   these to run off the end of.

   What it is *not* is a way around the borrow rule. A fixed array handed to a class function is a
   borrow like any other and the callee may not keep it - see `assumedRetained` in
   compiler/resolve/analyze_escape.cpp - which is what `Hmac.of` relies on to hand `update` a view of
   this pad.

   Sixty-four because that is a block of every digest in this module and is also at least as wide as
   any of their results - `Whirlpool`'s sixty-four is the largest - so one buffer serves both of the
   two things HMAC needs one for.
-}
pub fn newPad() -> [U8 *64] = [0]

{-
   The pad's bytes exclusive-or'd with a constant. `0x36` builds the inner pad and `0x36 ^ 0x5c`
   turns it into the outer one, which is why there is one function and not two.

   **A vector transfer and one written in portable source**, which is the pattern `Core/Reduce.yana`
   proves and the thing this file was not using: `loadVector` and `storeVector` have bodies on both
   targets (Implementation-Vector.md §12), so the same lines are `ymm` operations natively and
   `lanes` element accesses on JS - where the sixty-four-iteration loop they replace was already
   `lanes` accesses per byte. It is the `for v in vectors(xs)` form that is native-only, because an
   `iter fn` yielding from inside a `for` captures its continuation and JS has no object to name for
   one; a straight walk over the transfers needs no continuation and so needs no split.

   **Written out rather than looped, because the count is known and the optimizer will not unroll
   it.** `lanes` is a compile-time constant, so every test below folds and exactly one arm survives:
   two `vpxor`/`vmovdqu` pairs at v3, four at v2 and on JS, one at v4, and no branch, no counter and
   no back edge in any of them. The loop this replaces was emitted as written - two iterations at v3
   with an `add`, a `cmp` and a `jl` around one useful operation - and it is worth 1.4% of an
   HMAC-SHA-256 at v2 and 0.4% at v3, measured against this form in
   `test/bench/hash/findings.md` §3.

   The trailing loop is what makes that a shortcut rather than an assumption: sixty-four is a
   multiple of every width a target has today and each of them ends at a `return`, so the loop is
   folded away on all three - but a narrower `Vec(U8)` than sixteen bytes would still be *covered*
   rather than silently leaving a tail of the pad unmasked.
-}
pub fn xorPad(&pad: [U8 *64], with: U8) -> {}:
    let mask = splat(with) :: Vec(U8)
    let step = lanes(mask) :: Size

    storeVector(pad, 0, xor(loadVector(pad, 0), mask))
    if step >= 64 then return {}

    storeVector(pad, step, xor(loadVector(pad, step), mask))
    if step >= 32 then return {}

    storeVector(pad, step * 2, xor(loadVector(pad, step * 2), mask))
    storeVector(pad, step * 3, xor(loadVector(pad, step * 3), mask))
    if step >= 16 then return {}

    let &i = step * 4
    while i < 64:
        storeVector(pad, i, xor(loadVector(pad, i), mask))
        i = i + step

-- The key into the pad, leaving the rest of it zero - which is the zero extension the specification
-- asks for, already there because `newPad` made it.
pub fn copyIntoPad(&pad: [U8 *64], from: [U8]) -> {}:
    copyBytes(pad, 0, from, 0, length(from))

{-
   One-shot hashing is a function per digest, written out beside each digest, rather than one
   function over the class. **The dependency runs `d -> n`**, so a result can never select a state
   type: `digestOf(bytes) :: Sha256Digest` has nothing to bind `d` from, and the width alone could
   not decide it even if it did - `Fingerprint` is four digests' result. `Hmac.of` below is the same
   problem answered the other way, with the state arriving as an argument, and either answer is one
   line per digest; these are the ones where the line is the whole function.
-}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reading and writing the machine words a digest is made of.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `readBigU32` and its seven siblings are **Core's**, beside `loadVector` in `Core/Container.yana`,
   and were this file's until the compiler had an answer for them on both targets. What lives here
   now is only the pair a digest's padding wants, which is a byte *count* laid out as a bit count and
   is not a word transfer at all.

   The move is not a tidying. Every format that is not text is made of these, so the natural home is
   the container library rather than the first caller - and the cost at each end is a target question
   neither of which portable source can state: natively a load and a `movbe`, and on JS a `DataView`
   accessor through a view built once per array. See that file, and `NativeOp::HostWordRead` for the
   JS measurements.

   What this file gained by the move is the 64-bit pair on JS, which `getBigUint64` answers in a
   single call. Whirlpool no longer needs it: its JS implementation reads two 32-bit words instead,
   for the reason `Whirlpool.yana` gives.
-}

{-
   The 64-bit length field, as the two 32-bit halves the padding actually wants.

   A byte count reaches its bit count by three shifts and nothing wider: the low word is `bytes shl
   3` truncated and the high word is `bytes shr 29`. `Size` is 64 bits natively and 32 on JS, so the
   high word is always zero there - which is correct, since a JS host array cannot hold 512 MB of
   message in the first place.
-}
pub fn writeBigLength(&to: [U8], at: Size, bytes: Size) -> {}:
    writeBigU32(to, at, truncate(bytes `shr` 29) :: U32)
    writeBigU32(to, at + 4, truncate(bytes `shl` 3) :: U32)

pub fn writeLittleLength(&to: [U8], at: Size, bytes: Size) -> {}:
    writeLittleU32(to, at, truncate(bytes `shl` 3) :: U32)
    writeLittleU32(to, at + 4, truncate(bytes `shr` 29) :: U32)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The block machinery - what four of the six digests share.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   **Every block digest in this module has a sixty-four-byte block** - MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256 and
   Whirlpool all do - so the buffer below is `[U8 *64]` rather than a const parameter. The one thing
   that does vary, how many bytes of trailing length the padding writes (eight for the SHA family
   and MD5, thirty-two for Whirlpool), is a member of the class rather than a shape.

   `Blocks(c)` is a *wrapper* and not a superclass: a class function returning `&self.field` cannot
   be assigned through, because only `Index.getMut` is wired into a place expression, so a shared
   buffer reached through an accessor would be readable and not writable. Wrapping the algorithm
   state instead puts the buffer in a record this module owns, where an ordinary field assignment
   reaches it.
-}
pub class Block(c):
  {-
     `blocks` blocks of sixty-four bytes each, starting at `at`, folded into the state. The *only*
     thing an algorithm has to write, and where all of the time goes.

     **A run of blocks and not one block**, because a hardware compression has a *setup*: the SHA
     extension wants the eight working words in two vector registers, in an order that is not the
     order they are stored in, so a per-block member would pay that conversion once per block where
     this pays it once per call. See `Digest/Hardware.sha.yana`.

     For the four portable compressions it makes no difference.

     A caller that has one block passes one, which is what `padFinal` does.
  -}
  fn compressBlocks(&self: c, from: [U8], at: Size, blocks: Size) -> {}

{-
   And the padding rule, which is a class of its own rather than two more members of `Block`.

   The split lets the non-cryptographic hashes below use the buffering without pretending to have a
   padding: Murmur3 and xxHash32 read their tail bytes directly and finalize with the message
   length, so `lengthBytes` and `writeMessageLength` would be two members they answer with values
   nothing reads. The four Merkle-Damgård digests join this and the two others do not, and
   `padFinal` below is constrained on it, which is where the difference is enforced.
-}
pub class (Block(c)) Padded(c):
  -- How many trailing bytes the padding spends on the message length. Eight everywhere except
  -- Whirlpool, which states a 256-bit length and uses thirty-two.
  fn lengthBytes(self: c) -> Size

  {-
     The length field itself, written into the padded block.

     Which end the bytes go is the algorithm's - MD5 says little and the others say big - so this
     writes the field rather than answering a number and letting the caller guess.

     **It is handed a byte count and not a bit count**, which is a JS decision: the specifications
     all state a 64-bit count of *bits*, and a `U64` on that target is a `bigint` and off the
     arithmetic path entirely (see `Hash(Double)` in `Core/Hash.yana` for the same argument). Every
     implementation below wants the two halves separately anyway - it writes two 32-bit words - and
     `bytes shl 3` and `bytes shr 29` are those two halves in the `Size` domain, which is an ordinary
     integer on both targets.
  -}
  fn writeMessageLength(self: c, &to: [U8], at: Size, bytes: Size) -> {}

pub data Blocks(c) {state: c, block: [U8 *64], used: Size, total: Size}

{-
   One constructor for all seven, and the one rule about it that is JavaScript's.

   **A fresh block buffer is the fill literal `[0]`**, and not a global constant: a fixed array is a
   host array on JS, and copying a global one into a record field emits the global itself rather
   than a copy, so every digest in a program would share one sixty-four-byte buffer. A fill literal
   is a fresh array at every site on both targets - natively `lowerUniformFill` collapses it into
   one block set.

   This used to be written out at each of the seven `newDigest` bodies, because the owned argument
   to a generic constructor was dropped on JS - the emitted call was `newBlocks({})` and the digest
   folded over a state of no words at all, so every answer was zeros. That is fixed, and the shared
   form is byte-identical in the emitted JavaScript. `test/resolve/JsGenericAggregate.yana` is the
   reduced shape it was found at and holds it fixed: a record generic in one parameter, a concrete
   record with a fixed-array field passed into it by an owning function, compiled through the erased
   ABI and run on both targets.
-}
pub fn newBlocks(->state: c) -> Blocks(c) = Blocks {state: state, block: [0], used: 0, total: 0}

{-
   Absorbing input - the loop every `update` is, written once.

   Three phases and the middle one is the one that matters: whole blocks are compressed **straight
   out of the caller's buffer**, so a one-shot hash of a megabyte copies nothing at all - where an
   implementation that buffers every block first makes a second pass over the whole message.

   The other two phases are the partial block at each end. A digest fed a byte at a time therefore
   costs one copy per byte and one compression per sixty-four, which is the shape a streaming caller
   wants and is what the buffer exists for.
-}
pub fn (Block(c)) absorb(&self: Blocks(c), from: [U8]) -> {}:
    let n = length(from)
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    self.total = self.total + n

    -- Top up a block already part-filled. If it does not fill, there is nothing else to do: the
    -- whole of this input is now in the buffer.
    if self.used != 0:
        let want = 64 - self.used
        let take = if n < want then n else want

        copyBytes(self.block, self.used, from, 0, take)
        self.used = self.used + take
        at = take

        if self.used < 64 then return {}

        compressBlocks(self.state, self.block, 0, 1)
        self.used = 0

    -- The bulk, read where the caller put it, in one call rather than one call per block: what a
    -- hardware compression does once per call it would otherwise do once per block.
    if at + 64 <= n:
        let whole = (n - at) / 64
        compressBlocks(self.state, from, at, whole)
        at = at + whole * 64

    -- And the tail, kept for the next call or for the padding.
    if at < n:
        copyBytes(self.block, 0, from, at, n - at)
        self.used = n - at

{-
   Padding, and the one place a digest's shape shows through.

   `0x80`, then zeros, then the message length - which is the Merkle-Damgård padding all four of
   these share. The length field is the class's to write and its width is the class's to state, so
   Whirlpool's thirty-two bytes need no special case here: what changes is where the zeros stop.

   The count is in *bits* and is taken modulo 2^64 by the type, which is what every one of these
   specifications says to do.
-}
pub fn (Padded(c)) padFinal(&self: Blocks(c)) -> {}:
    let bytes = self.total
    let tail = lengthBytes(self.state)

    self.block[self.used] = 128
    self.used = self.used + 1

    -- No room left for the length in this block, so it is filled with zeros and compressed first.
    if self.used + tail > 64:
        zeroBytes(self.block, self.used, 64 - self.used)
        compressBlocks(self.state, self.block, 0, 1)
        self.used = 0

    zeroBytes(self.block, self.used, 64 - tail - self.used)
    writeMessageLength(self.state, self.block, 64 - tail, bytes)
    compressBlocks(self.state, self.block, 0, 1)
    self.used = 0

{-
   The state words, as the digest they spell - the second half of every `finish` in this module, and
   the half that was written out three times.

   **Two counts and neither of them is a number here**: `w` is how many words the state holds and `n`
   how many bytes the digest is, and the two are not the same question - SHA-224 carries eight words
   and answers twenty-eight bytes, so the loop stops at the *digest's* width and the last word is
   half spent. `n` is bound by the expected type, which is the result of the `finish` that calls
   this; nothing else decides it, and nothing else has to.

   Big-endian and 32-bit because that is what SHA-1, SHA-224 and SHA-256 are. MD5 writes the same
   shape little-endian and Whirlpool writes eight 64-bit words, and both keep their own loop rather
   than turning this into a function with a mode: one caller each, and an endianness argument would
   be read at every call to save four lines at one of them.
-}
pub fn (n: Int, w: Int) bigU32Digest(h: [U32 *w]) -> [U8 *n]:
    let &out = [0] :: [U8 *n]
    let width = n :: Size

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i * 4 < width:
        writeBigU32(out, i * 4, h[i])
        i = i + 1

    return out

{-
   The two block operations every buffer in this file is filled through.

   **They were two byte loops, and the comment that held them there was wrong.** It said a call into
   the block ladder would cost more in dispatch than the loop costs outright, on the grounds that
   nothing here runs over more than sixty-four bytes. What that missed is what `copyMemory` *is*:
   not a call to the machine's block instruction but a ladder whose rungs are register transfers,
   which at these lengths is the two-load two-store rung and no loop at all - against sixty-four
   iterations of a bounds-checked subscript pair. `Native/Memory.native.yana` is the ladder and
   `test/bench/memmove/findings.md` is what each rung measured.

   **Two implementations, because a JS build has no bytes.** `copyMemory` and `setMemory` reach that
   target only in the shape the relocation glue writes (`blockCopyShape` in codegen/js/type.cpp),
   which is a whole value and not a counted run - so the loop is what that target has, and it is the
   loop this pair always was. The split is the one Analysis-Modules.md §2.5 describes and it stays
   in this file rather than becoming two: five lines apiece, one of them shared.

   **Non-overlapping, which `copyMemory` requires and every caller here already promises**: a block
   is topped up from the caller's message, a pad is filled from a digest that was just finished, and
   no two of those are ever the same storage. `moveMemory` is the one that establishes it instead of
   asking, and nothing here needs to.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn copyBytes(&to: [U8], toAt: Size, from: [U8], fromAt: Size,
                                   count: Size) -> {} =
    copyMemory(to.items + toAt, from.items + fromAt, count)

@platform(native) pub fn zeroBytes(&to: [U8], at: Size, count: Size) -> {} =
    setMemory(to.items + at, 0, count)

@platform(js) pub fn copyBytes(&to: [U8], toAt: Size, from: [U8], fromAt: Size, count: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        to[toAt + i] = from[fromAt + i]
        i = i + 1

@platform(js) pub fn zeroBytes(&to: [U8], at: Size, count: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        to[at + i] = 0
        i = i + 1

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   HMAC - RFC 2104, once.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `H((K ^ opad) || H((K ^ ipad) || message))`, written once over the class, with a name per digest
   in front of it.

   **The names are a namespace rather than a prefix** - `Hmac.sha1` where four spellings of
   `hmacSha1` used to be. A namespace and not a module (doc/spec/modules.md): these four are a thin
   face over `Hmac.of` and share every one of its internals, so a module would charge them a `pub`
   boundary with nothing behind it and charge every caller a second import to reach a name
   `import Digest` already brings. What the prefix buys is the reading - `Hmac.sha1(key, message)`
   beside `digestOf(bytes) :: Sha1` says which of the two a call is.

   **The state is the argument, because `d -> n` runs the wrong way for a result to select one.**
   `Hmac.of(key, message) :: Sha256Digest` has nothing to bind `d` from - and `Fingerprint` is four
   digests' result here, so the dependency could not be turned round either. So the digest arrives as
   a value whose type says which one it is, which is `HMac<Sha256>` said in the one place Yana has to
   say it, and the four names below are what a caller actually writes.

   **What a generic body may hand a class function** is the reason this was four bodies until the
   borrow rule was stated: a deferred class dispatch has no summary, and assuming every argument was
   kept meant `update(inner, message)` marked the caller's slice as outliving the frame. What stands
   in for the missing summary now is the signature - a borrow's extent is the call unless a body says
   otherwise - and `checkClassBorrows` holds every implementation of `update` to it. See
   `assumedRetained` in `compiler/resolve/analyze_escape.cpp`.

   **The class's width is what makes it generic.** `finish` answers `[U8 *n]` and `n` is a number
   this body has, so the inner digest is copied into the pad here rather than through a second class
   member that existed only to be routed by `d`. When the result was an opaque `h`, neither its bytes
   nor its length were reachable - `Contiguous(h, U8)` is the instance that cannot be written, for
   the reason the note beside `Length([a *n])` in `Core/Container.yana` gives - and `finishInto` was
   nine copies of the three lines that closed it.

   **The outer pad goes in before the inner digest comes out**, which is the one thing about the
   order that is not the specification's: the two pads differ by `0x36 ^ 0x5c`, so the second is the
   first flipped rather than the key expanded again - and once the outer digest has read it, the
   buffer is free for the inner digest to be finished into. That is what keeps this to one
   sixty-four-byte pad instead of two.

   A key longer than a block is replaced by its own digest, which is the specification's rule and
   the one place HMAC hashes three times instead of twice. It goes straight into the pad, whose
   remaining bytes are already the zeros the extension wants.

   `Hmac.whirlpool` is the widest of them and the one whose digest exactly fills the pad, which is
   the case `newPad`'s sixty-four was chosen for.
-}
pub fn (Digest(d, n)) Hmac.of(->state: d, key: [U8], message: [U8]) -> [U8 *n]:
    let &pad = newPad()
    let width = n :: Size

    if length(key) <= 64:
        copyIntoPad(pad, key)
    else:
        let &folding = newDigest() :: d
        update(folding, key)
        copyBytes(pad, 0, finish(folding), 0, width)

    let &inner = state
    xorPad(pad, 54)
    update(inner, pad)
    update(inner, message)

    let &outer = newDigest() :: d
    xorPad(pad, 106)
    update(outer, pad)

    copyBytes(pad, 0, finish(inner), 0, width)
    update(outer, pad[0..truncate(width) :: Int])
    return finish(outer)

pub fn Hmac.md5(key: [U8], message: [U8]) -> Md5Digest =
    Hmac.of(newDigest() :: Md5, key, message)

pub fn Hmac.sha1(key: [U8], message: [U8]) -> Sha1Digest =
    Hmac.of(newDigest() :: Sha1, key, message)

pub fn Hmac.sha224(key: [U8], message: [U8]) -> Sha224Digest =
    Hmac.of(newDigest() :: Sha224, key, message)

pub fn Hmac.whirlpool(key: [U8], message: [U8]) -> WhirlpoolDigest =
    Hmac.of(newDigest() :: Whirlpool, key, message)

pub fn Hmac.sha256(key: [U8], message: [U8]) -> Sha256Digest =
    Hmac.of(newDigest() :: Sha256, key, message)
